y + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n    97\t| D-047 | 2026-05-10 | Adopt WP-SCP-024 024B-extras operational contracts: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` restores branch protection from the captured prior-state log entry by transforming GET-shape JSON into the PUT body, stripping envelope fields recursively, converting `enforce_admins.enabled` to a boolean, and replaying `required_signatures` through the dedicated sub-resource; restore-mode posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-replaces-required-check-context`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, and `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions` are mandatory when the target removes or re-enables those protections, and the restore-mode posture guard now fires on either empty `required_status_checks.contexts` or a non-canonical context set (both require an explicit acknowledgement flag); forward-mode now performs a path-pinned workflow lookup for `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml`, queries workflow-runs without a branch filter, validates `--expected-wrapper-sha` against actual release tags via `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/git/refs/tags/<tag>` with annotated-tag dereference fallback, and, when the adopter wrapper file is readable, verifies that `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml` pins the same SHA before Gate 3 completes; inaccessible wrapper content now requires an explicit `--i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible` acknowledgement rather than silently degrading to a CAUTION-only audit note; the new CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` wires the CLI gate into PR checks with `paths: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**` plus explicit branch-protection-log.md / STATUS.md triggers and tooling-slice short-circuiting; ADOPT-001 gains §12.8 break-glass procedure and explicitly names the `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` escape hatch; STATUS.md and the DISPATCH-NOTE placeholder for the step-7 operator demo are updated in-slice. | ACCEPTED | The 024B split left the restore / break-glass / workflow-integration surface as the deferred half so the review load stayed bounded. This decision ratifies the operational contract that lets operators disable and re-enable the federation primitive safely without widening the adopter-facing API surface, while preserving the bootstrap guard that prevents tooling slices from self-blocking their own PRs. The release-tag SHA requirement is intentionally anchored to actual git tags, not arbitrary commits, so Gate 3 can re-arm against a concrete release artifact rather than against moving main-head. After any break-glass cycle, `prior_restore_evidence_present` scans the full git history, so every subsequent forward-mode re-enable for the same adopter permanently requires `--expected-wrapper-sha` unless the operator deliberately takes the audited Gate 2 bypass; that permanence is intentional. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, the escape hatch is explicitly auditable, and the restore demo evidence is still an operator-interactive step outside Codex dispatch. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras authoring time. |\n    98\t\n    99\t> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n\n---\n     1\t# SCP Federation Primitive — Versioning Contract\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status:** active as of slice 020H.1 (2026-05-01).\n     4\t**Closes:** WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 sub-criteria (i) + (ii).\n     5\t\n     6\t---\n     7\t\n     8\t## Scope\n     9\t\n    10\tThis document defines the semver contract that the SCP federation\n    11\tprimitive guarantees to adopters who pin a `# tag: v<X>.<Y>.<Z>`\n    12\twrapper at `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml`. It covers three\n    13\tpublic surfaces:\n    14\t\n    15\t1. **Reusable workflow inputs** — `workflow_call.inputs` declared on\n    16\t   `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml`.\n    17\t2. **Schema contracts** — JSON Schema at\n    18\t   `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` (the structured\n    19\t   summary artefact every adopter consumes for nightly + release-gate\n    20\t   reconciliation), `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, and\n    21\t   `schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` (the opt-in cross-repo\n    22\t   scorecard emit per WP-SCP-023 023B / D-041 — added at v1.2.0;\n    23\t   MAJOR-pinned per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1).\n    24\t3. **Rule IDs** — the `SCP-R-NNN` identifiers under `policies/`.\n    25\t4. **Opt-in workflow inputs** — `scorecard-emit: bool` (added at\n    26\t   v1.2.0 per WP-SCP-023 023B; default false). Opt-in inputs follow\n    27\t   the same MINOR/MAJOR semver category as core inputs.\n    28\t\n    29\tIt does NOT cover internal-only surfaces: `lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`,\n    30\t`scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, the\n    31\tRenovate preset internal shape, the OPA Rego `scp_common.rego`\n    32\thelpers, or the SCP-self cross-repo aggregator pipeline\n    33\t(`.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml`,\n    34\t`schemas/scorecard-opt-in-registry.schema.json`,\n    35\t`schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`,\n    36\t`docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml`,\n    37\t`output/scorecards/index.json` — added in slice 023C / D-042; mirrors\n    38\t`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` posture per slice 020N).\n    39\tThese are refactored without notice.\n    40\t\n    41\t---\n    42\t\n    43\t## Semver guarantee\n    44\t\n    45\t**`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`** with strict semantic-versioning meaning:\n    46\t\n    47\t| Bump | When | Adopter action required |\n    48\t|---|---|---|\n    49\t| **PATCH** (`v1.0.x`) | Bug fixes, new informational annotations, performance improvements, observability records, internal refactors of helpers/lib. | None — drop in the SHA bump and merge. |\n    50\t| **MINOR** (`v1.x.0`) | New `workflow_call` inputs (with safe defaults), new `SCP-R-NNN` rules added with `threshold: warn` baseline, new optional schema fields, new commit-status contexts (informational), new `disabled_rules:` reasons. | Recommended: review the release notes for new rules. None mandatory unless the adopter wants to opt into a new rule's `threshold: deny`. |\n    51\t| **MAJOR** (`vX.0.0`) | Breaking changes — see \"What is breaking\" below. | **Mandatory** — read the migration guide in the release notes; an amending decision row in adopter's `DECISIONS.md` (or equivalent) before bumping the SHA pin. |\n    52\t\n    53\t---\n    54\t\n    55\t## What counts as a breaking change\n    56\t\n    57\tAny of the following requires a MAJOR bump and an amending decision row:\n    58\t\n    59\t### Workflow inputs\n    60\t\n    61\t- Removing a `workflow_call.inputs.<name>`.\n    62\t- Renaming a `workflow_call.inputs.<name>`.\n    63\t- Changing the `default:` value of an input in a way that flips an existing adopter's effective behaviour (e.g. `threshold: warn` → `threshold: deny`).\n    64\t- Changing the `type:` of an input.\n    65\t- Making a previously-optional input required.\n    66\t\n    67\t### Schema contracts\n    68\t\n    69\t- Removing a previously-required field from `policy-check-summary.schema.json` or `rule-config.schema.json`.\n    70\t- Renaming any field (use add-new-then-deprecate instead).\n    71\t- Tightening a string `pattern:` such that previously-valid values are rejected.\n    72\t- Tightening a `format:` constraint (e.g. `date` → `date-time`).\n    73\t- Adding a new required field (covered by the deprecation ramp — see below).\n    74\t- Removing an `enum:` value that was previously emitted.\n    75\t\n    76\t### Rule IDs\n    77\t\n    78\t- Removing an `SCP-R-NNN` rule.\n    79\t- Re-numbering an `SCP-R-NNN` rule (the ID is the durable contract; the underlying logic is internal).\n    80\t- Promoting a rule from `threshold: warn` to `threshold: deny` as the **default** at the SCP source. (Adopters may always override per-rule via `.scp/rule-config.yaml`; the default flip is what's breaking.)\n    81\t- Tightening a rule's match scope such that previously-passing manifests now deny.\n    82\t\n    83\t### Read-back / annotation contracts\n    84\t\n    85\t- Removing `scp/policy-check-readback` commit-status posting.\n    86\t- Removing a `::warning::` or `::error::` annotation surface that adopters' downstream tooling parses (e.g. an annotation parser that grouped on `title=SCP-E001`).\n    87\t- Renaming an `SCP-ENNN` error-code identifier.\n    88\t\n    89\t### Branch-protection helper\n    90\t\n    91\t- Removing `--repo`, `--branch`, `--plan`, `--no-enforce-admins`, `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate`, `--restore`, `--expected-wrapper-sha`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-replaces-required-check-context`, `--i-understand-restore-disables-required-signatures`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions`, `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci`, `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification`, or `--i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible` flags from `scripts/enable-required-check.sh`.\n    92\t- Changing the script's invocation-log markdown block schema.\n    93\t\n    94\t### Tag protection\n    95\t\n    96\t- Changing the `v*` or `renovate/v*` Repository Ruleset shape (deletion / non-fast-forward / update behaviour).\n    97\t\n    98\t---\n    99\t\n   100\t## What does NOT count as breaking\n   101\t\n   102\t- Adding new `workflow_call.inputs.<name>` with a safe default.\n   103\t- Adding new `SCP-R-NNN` rules at `threshold: warn` baseline.\n   104\t- Adding new schema fields (`additionalProperties: false` is preserved — but new fields are added under existing properties, not as required new top-level fields).\n   105\t- Adding new `enum:` values that were not previously emitted.\n   106\t- Adding new `disabled_rules:` reasons.\n   107\t- Adding new informational `::warning::` or `::error::` titles (if existing titles preserved).\n   108\t- Internal helper-library refactors.\n   109\t- New commit-status contexts (the existing two are stable).\n   110\t- New OPA / Conftest / Regal binary version bumps in the lockfile (these are SHA-pinned; adopters get the new pins via Renovate auto-bump).\n   111\t\n   112\t---\n   113\t\n   114\t## Deprecation ramp (one-release warning window)\n   115\t\n   116\t**Closes 020H.1 sub-criterion (ii); enforced by 020H.3 release-gate.**\n   117\t\n   118\tWhen the SCP source needs to remove or breaking-change one of the\n   119\tpublic surfaces above, the change MUST proceed via a **one-release\n   120\tdeprecation ramp**:\n   121\t\n   122\t1. **Release N: add the new surface** (alongside the old surface), and\n   123\t   in the same release add a `::warning::` annotation to the old\n   124\t   surface stating: `<surface> deprecated; use <new-surface>; will be\n   125\t   removed in v<X+1>.0.0`. Document in release notes. **Add an entry\n   126\t   to `policies/deprecations.yaml`** with `announced_at`,\n   127\t   `announced_release`, `target_release`, and `migration_pointer`\n   128\t   (per `schemas/deprecations.schema.json`).\n   129\t2. **Release N+1 (the next MINOR or MAJOR — whichever lands first):**\n   130\t   keep both surfaces live; the warning continues to fire.\n   131\t3. **Release N+M (the MAJOR after the next MINOR):** old surface is\n   132\t   removed; the MAJOR bump is the breaking-change vehicle. An amending\n   133\t   decision row in `docs/DECISIONS.md` records the removal. **Remove\n   134\t   the entry from `policies/deprecations.yaml`** as part of the\n   135\t   removal commit.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe minimum gap between deprecation announcement (step 1) and\n   138\tremoval (step 3) is **one MINOR release**. Adopters who track every\n   139\tMINOR release see at least one full PR cycle's worth of warnings\n   140\tbefore the surface vanishes.\n   141\t\n   142\t**Machine enforcement** — two operating modes per `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml`:\n   143\t\n   144\t1. **Dry-run pre-flight (workflow_dispatch).** The pre-emptive\n   145\t   enforcement path. The operator runs the gate BEFORE pushing the\n   146\t   tag:\n   147\t\n   148\t   ```bash\n   149\t   gh workflow run release-gate.yml -f dry_run_tag=v1.1.0\n   150\t   # Wait for the run to start, then watch for completion + non-zero\n   151\t   # exit on failure (gh run list always exits 0; gh run watch with\n   152\t   # --exit-status surfaces the workflow's verdict). Closes 020H.3 R2\n   153\t   # nit-SAFE-001.\n   154\t   sleep 5  # allow the dispatch to register\n   155\t   RUN_ID=\"$(gh run list --workflow=release-gate.yml --limit 1 --json databaseId --jq '.[0].databaseId')\"\n   156\t   gh run watch \"${RUN_ID}\" --exit-status\n   157\t   ```\n   158\t\n   159\t   If the dry-run exits clean, the tag is safe to push. **The\n   160\t   dry-run pre-flight is mandatory before every v* tag-cut.**\n   161\t\n   162\t2. **Post-tag observer (`push: tags: ['v*']`).** GitHub Actions\n   163\t   cannot block a `push: tags` event — by the time the workflow\n   164\t   fires, the tag already exists on the remote. This trigger\n   165\t   therefore acts as a last-line-of-defense observer: it annotates\n   166\t   the bad tag with `SCP-EREL-001` AND opens a\n   167\t   `release-gate-violation` GitHub issue auto-assigned to `@jrnb2024`\n   168\t   for triage (per slice 020H.3.1 closure of TF-020H3rg-003 —\n   169\t   `permissions: { issues: write }` on the workflow + de-dup by tag,\n   170\t   matching the canary-replay.yml pattern). The bad tag itself is\n   171\t   immutable per D-030 (the `scp-tag-protection-v` Repository\n   172\t   Ruleset blocks deletion + force-push + non-fast-forward, including\n   173\t   for admins).\n   174\t\n   175\tBoth modes evaluate the same TWO invariants:\n   176\t\n   177\t- any `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry has `target_release` matching\n   178\t  the candidate tag AND `announced_release` is not at least one MINOR\n   179\t  behind the candidate, OR\n   180\t- any `.scp/rule-config.yaml` entry on the SCP repo itself has\n   181\t  `disable: true` AND `expires_at < <UTC date>` (the SCP-self\n   182\t  half of TF-005 — adopter-side rule-config expiry is enforced at\n   183\t  PR time via SCP-E007, not here).\n   184\t\n   185\tThe release-gate also emits **warning** annotations\n   186\t(`SCP-EREL-001-warn`) on suspicious deprecation patterns\n   187\t(announced_release more than one major behind candidate) without\n   188\tblocking — operators triage manually.\n   189\t\n   190\t### Tag-cut procedure (operator)\n   191\t\n   192\tMandatory pre-flight + apply pattern. ALWAYS:\n   193\t\n   194\t1. **Author the release-prep PR.** Bump `version-manifest.json`;\n   195\t   add or remove entries in `policies/deprecations.yaml` per the\n   196\t   ramp; write release notes; merge to `main`.\n   197\t2. **Dry-run the gate.** `gh workflow run release-gate.yml -f dry_run_tag=<tag>` and wait for exit 0.\n   198\t3. **Push the tag.** Only after dry-run is clean. The post-tag\n   199\t   observer fires automatically as a final check.\n   200\t4. **Publish the GitHub release** + Renovate bump cascade per the\n   201\t   normal 020H part 2 procedure.\n   202\t\n   203\t### Bad-tag recovery procedure\n   204\t\n   205\tIf a tag is pushed that fails the post-tag observer:\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Do NOT attempt to delete or force-push the tag.** The D-030 +\n   208\t  D-034 tag-protection rulesets block deletion and force-push,\n   209\t  including for admins. The bad tag is immutable.\n   210\t- **Cut a corrected `v<X>.<Y+1>.0`** (next MINOR) that satisfies\n   211\t  the ramp. The bad tag remains in the tag list but is superseded\n   212\t  by the corrected one.\n   213\t- **Add a release note on the bad tag's GitHub release page**\n   214\t  documenting the violation + pointing adopters to the corrected\n   215\t  tag. Detection latency depends on the adopter's bump path:\n   216\t  - **Renovate-using adopters** (the §12.7.2 recommended path) see\n   217\t    the automated bump PR to `v<X>.<Y+1>.0` within hours of\n   218\t    publication; the bump PR is the primary signal.\n   219\t  - **Manually SHA-pinning adopters** see no immediate signal at\n   220\t    all. The `SCP-FRESH-001` freshness warning (ADOPT-001 §12.7.11)\n\n---\n     1\t# Standards Control Plane — STATUS\n     2\t\n     3\t**Last updated:** 2026-05-11 (WP-SCP-024 024B-extras fix-round-11; restore CAUTION rendering + Gate 2/3 hardening + reqcheck TF procedure update)\n     4\t\n     5\t## At-a-glance\n     6\t\n     7\t| Programme | State | Track |\n     8\t|---|---|---|\n     9\t| WP-SCP-019 (Service Auth Contract) | ✅ closed 2026-04-20 | — |\n    10\t| WP-SCP-020 (Policy Federation Primitive) | ✅ **closed 2026-04-30** at v1.0.0 (Threshold A) | Track 1 |\n    11\t| WP-SCP-021 (MCP Server) | ✅ landed 2026-04-29 (USER-GATE-C signed) | Track 2 |\n    12\t| WP-SCP-022 (Implementation Programme) | ✅ **closed 2026-04-30** (USER-GATE-A signed) | Meta |\n    13\t\n    14\t## Threshold A — REACHED 2026-04-30\n    15\t\n    16\t> **Threshold A definition:** SCP gates itself on its own `main` via the federation primitive's reusable workflow with a required-status-check, all three v1.0.0 Rego rules enforcing, conflict-gate green on every PR, and a v1.0.0 release tag cut.\n    17\t\n    18\t```\n    19\t020A    plan + D-022 + D-023                                     ✅ landed\n    20\t020B    reusable workflow                                        ✅ landed (PR #36)\n    21\t020B.1  workflow-selftest harness                                ✅ landed (PR #38)\n    22\t020B.2  scripts/scp-policy-check local repro                     ✅ landed (PR #41)\n    23\t020C    starter Rego rule library (3 rules)                      ✅ landed (PR #49, 2026-04-28)\n    24\t020C.1  waiver-aware Rego + conflict-gate + read-back            ✅ landed (PR #52, 2026-04-29)\n    25\t020J    tag-protection v* + required-signed-commits              ✅ landed (PR #53, 2026-04-30) + applied\n    26\t020K    CODEOWNERS wiring (personal-account / single-operator)   ✅ landed (PR #56, 2026-04-30)\n    27\t020D1   self-dogfood wrapper merged (advisory mode)              ✅ landed (PR #57, 2026-04-30)\n    28\t020H part 1  v1.0.0-rc.1 release notes + tag cut                 ✅ landed (PR #58, 2026-04-30)\n    29\t020E.a  pre-protection canary                                    ✅ landed (PR #60, 2026-04-30)\n    30\t🛑 USER-GATE-A0  human signoff before promoting to required       ✅ signed (PR #61, 2026-04-30)\n    31\t020H part 2  promote v1.0.0-rc.1 → v1.0.0                        ✅ landed (PR #62, 2026-04-30) + tag cut\n    32\t020D2   flip gate to required (enforce_admins=true)              ✅ landed (PR #63, 2026-04-30) + applied\n    33\t020D2.1 reconciliation (D-033)                                   ✅ landed (this PR, 2026-04-30)\n    34\t🛑 USER-GATE-A  Threshold A signoff (FINISH LINE)                 ✅ **signed** (PR #64, 2026-04-30)\n    35\t```\n    36\t\n    37\t## Live operational state\n    38\t\n    39\t- `enforce_admins: true` on `main` — admin override OFF.\n    40\t- `required_status_checks: [\"policy-check / scp/policy-check\"]` (strict).\n    41\t- `required_signatures: true` on every commit reaching `main`.\n    42\t- `required_pull_request_reviews: count=0, codeowner=false` (single-operator mode per D-033).\n    43\t- Tag-protection ruleset on `v*` (deletion / non_fast_forward / update blocked).\n    44\t- Tag-protection ruleset on `renovate/v*` (deletion / non_fast_forward / update blocked) — added 2026-04-30 with 020F (D-034).\n    45\t- Renovate shared preset live at `renovate/default.json`, tagged `renovate/v1.0.0`; SCP self consumes the preset via `renovate.json` pinned to `#renovate/v1.0.0`.\n    46\t- `v1.0.0` released at `https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/releases/tag/v1.0.0`.\n    47\t- `v1.0.1` released at `https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/releases/tag/v1.0.1` (2026-05-02, slice 020M) — Python deps hash-pinned via `requirements/policy-check.txt`; PATCH bump, no public-surface change.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Today's chain (2026-04-30 — single session)\n    50\t\n    51\t13 PRs merged + 2 release tags + 1 GitHub release published. WP-SCP-022 reaches Threshold A. WP-SCP-020 closed.\n    52\t\n    53\t| # | PR | Slice | Outcome |\n    54\t|---|---|---|---|\n    55\t| 1 | #53 | 020J | tag-protection + required_signatures applied